  "answer_text": "**Yes, Coca-Cola did originally contain cocaine.** \n\nThe history of how cocaine ended up in the world's most famous soft drink\u2014and how it is still connected to the drug today\u2014is a fascinating look into 19th-century medicine and modern manufacturing.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Original Recipe\nIn 1885, Atlanta pharmacist Dr. John Stith Pemberton created the syrup for Coca-Cola. At the time, cocaine was entirely legal and commonly used in medicines, tonics, and over-the-counter elixirs. \n\nPemberton named his creation after its two key active ingredients:\n* **\"Coca\"** from the extract of South American **coca leaves** (which contained cocaine).\n* **\"Cola\"** from the **kola nut**, a West African fruit that provided caffeine.\n\nIn the late 1800s, a single glass of Coca-Cola contained an active, albeit relatively small, dose of cocaine\u2014estimated to be roughly **9 milligrams per serving**. Because of this, it was marketed as a patent medicine that could cure headaches, relieve exhaustion, and soothe nerves.\n\n### 2. Why and When was Cocaine Removed?\nBy the turn of the 20th century, public and scientific understanding of cocaine shifted. It went from being viewed as a \"miracle cure\" to a highly addictive, dangerous substance. Additionally, in the American South, racially charged fears and moral panic about cocaine use among Black Americans put intense pressure on the company. \n\nTo protect its image, Coca-Cola began phasing out the drug:\n* **In 1903**, the company transitioned to using **decocainized (spent) coca leaves**. This removed the active drug while retaining the distinct flavor of the coca leaf.\n* By the time the U.S. passed the Pure Food and Drug Act of 1906, Coca-Cola was entirely cocaine-free. \n\n---\n\n### 3. The Modern Connection: How Coca-Cola is Made Today\nThough Coca-Cola hasn't contained the actual drug for over a century, **it still uses the coca plant today**. \n\nTo maintain the drink's original flavor profile, Coca-Cola uses a highly specialized supply chain:\n1. **The Stepan Company** (based in Maywood, New Jersey) is the **only company in the United States authorized by the DEA to import coca leaves**. They import roughly 100 metric tons of dried coca leaves from Peru each year.\n2. At their facility, Stepan extracts the cocaine alkaloid from the leaves, leaving behind a \"decocainized\" leaf extract.\n3. **The Flavor:** The decocainized extract is shipped directly to Coca-Cola to serve as a flavoring agent in the soda syrup.\n4. **The Cocaine:** The extracted active cocaine is not destroyed. Instead, it is legally sold to Mallinckrodt, a pharmaceutical company, which processes it into a prescription local anesthetic used by doctors and dentists (often for eye, nose, or throat surgeries).",
